window.MOB3_SEARCH = [{"path":"index.html","title":"MOB³ is a small indie web browser for the terminal","text":"MOB³ is a small indie web browser for the terminal MOB3 is a lightweight terminal web browser with a built-in HTML rendering engine, JavaScript runtime, ANSI renderer and inline image support. It brings Vim-style interaction, mouse controls and browser-like navigation together with headless MCP support for scripts and AI agents. The design goal is most of the features people actually use, with a fraction of the CPU and memory a mainstream browser needs: a small static binary, fast startup and pages that stay lightweight. Project page: mob3.net GitHub: github.com/mob3net/mob3 Documentation Documentation index Use cases — developers, AI agents, terminal users and automation Architecture — process model, package boundaries and design tradeoffs Install — releases, Docker and source builds Command line — every flag, default and limit Keyboard and mouse Automation with MCP — headless control for scripts and AI tools Security and privacy , site support and development Try it with Docker: docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mob3net/mob3 -graphics blocks Download and install Download the latest release from GitHub Releases. Release archives cover Linux ( 386 , amd64 , armv6 , armv7 , arm64 ) and macOS ( amd64 , arm64 ). Choose the archive for your OS and architecture, for example mob3_vX.Y.Z_linux_amd64.tar.gz . Each archive contains the static mob3 binary. Example: tar -xzf mob3_vX.Y.Z_linux_amd64.tar.gz sudo install -m 0755 mob3 /usr/local/bin/mob3 mob3 -version Screenshots Show your support If MOB3 has been useful to you, please consider giving the project a star on GitHub . It helps others discover it and supports the continued development of the project. What to expect MOB3 is a content browser, not a full browser engine. It is happiest with text-focused pages and gets rough on modern JavaScript-heavy apps. Works well Documentation, blogs, news articles, wikis, forums, changelogs and old or server-rendered HTML. Headings, paragraphs, lists, links, tables, <pre> blocks, images and common presentational markup. Text/search inputs, textareas, single-select option popups and multi-select list boxes, with a visible caret, click-to-place caret, arrow-key editing, date/time/month/week, range sliders, color swatches, HTML constraint checks, GET and urlencoded POST form submission. The start page has justified text and a full-width search/URL box that resolves input the same way as the o prompt, including the -search fallback. Common CSS: selectors, cascade and inheritance, box model (including box-sizing and min/max sizes), margins, borders, background colors and background-image photos on block containers, text styling, lists, tables, CSS custom properties, viewport-width @media queries, viewport units, calc() and min() / max() / clamp() , basic flex rows with alignment and ordering, left/right floats with line wrapping and clear , and overflow clipping for bounded panels. PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP/BMP/SVG/data-URI images when enabled with -graphics ( auto , kitty , iterm , or blocks for half-block art); press i to cycle full graphics, blocks and none. Images are off by default for speed. Session-only memory cache for CSS, scripts, image responses and decoded images, budgeted at 3% of -mem-limit (or 32 MiB when unlimited). In -process=per-tab , -mem-limit applies per process, so each tab child, an MCP engine child and the parent each get their own limit. Environment proxy support through HTTP_PROXY , HTTPS_PROXY and NO_PROXY , including HTTP, HTTPS and SOCKS5 proxies; there is no proxy settings UI. In-memory back/forward cache: Back and Forward restore the page, its DOM and its JavaScript state without a network reload and pause its timers while cached. The cache is memory-aware — a history budget of 12% of -mem-limit (or 128 MB when unlimited) evicts the least recently used pages by estimated size, and pages heavier than half the budget are not cached at all — so heavy sites cannot pin memory. Nothing is written to disk. Light JavaScript: DOM queries and mutation, classList / style , click handlers, timers, alert/confirm/prompt and native <dialog> open/close, same-origin fetch / XMLHttpRequest plus simple CORS cross-origin requests, and in-memory storage/cookies. Script errors turn the footer JavaScript label into a red JavaScript:Error ; J or clicking that label toggles scripting for the session and reloads the page, while ! opens the console. JavaScript console ( ! ): tabbed view of logs, requests, cookies and in-memory storage, the raw page HTML, and a Tools tab for read-only page inspection ( help , form , links , requests , logs , source , snapshot , history , clear ); evaluate one-line expressions in the page's runtime, with Up/Down or Ctrl-P/Ctrl-N history and results appended to the log. clear / cls empties either the JS log or the Tools scrollback, and the × in the console menu closes it. Reader mode ( R , the footer Reader:on / Reader:off label, or -reader at launch): extracts the main article into a narrow, chrome-free text column, stays enabled across navigation, and restores the normal page and scroll position when toggled off. Built-in text-only PDF viewer: a PDF response is extracted page by page into one continuous document with column alignment, a centered reading column and a Page n caption above an underscore rule after each page; every page is padded to the same height so it scrolls like a book, and two-column pages are read column by column. PDF headings keep their spacing, and runs clearly larger than the body text use the same 32px headline path (two rows, scaled glyphs in kitty) as HTML headings. Find, selection, -dump and -png work as on any page. The footer shows Page n/N with a clickable (S -> Download name) hint, and S opens the same curl popup. -png exports one page ( -pdf-page=N or all for the whole document). Figures, images and annotations are not rendered; -no-pdf restores the download prompt. Dark mode ( D , the footer Dark:on / Dark:off label, or -dark at launch): asks sites for their dark styles with prefers-color-scheme: dark . Pages that already render a dark background keep their own palette; otherwise backgrounds, text, links and borders are remapped to a dark palette. Images keep their original colors. Page zoom ( + / - , 0 resets, or -zoom N at launch): changes how many CSS pixels a cell represents, so fixed-pixel layout, spacing, images and kitty scaled large headings grow or shrink while body text keeps its size and line height. The CSS viewport follows the ratio, so responsive pages reflow. Zoom is session-only; the URL row shows the current level, and clicking that badge opens a window for choosing a preset with the mouse. Vimium-style link hints ( f ): highlights letters from each visible link or form control, then follows the link or activates the control when you type them. F opens the chosen link in a background tab. p previews the destination, with Enter to follow and y to copy. Middle-clicking a link opens it in the background; right-clicking a link opens the same preview. Esc or scrolling dismisses the hints. Headless MCP automation ( -mcp ): navigate, snapshot page text, list links and forms, click links or elements by text, fill forms, select options, submit, wait for async content, type, press keys, scroll, evaluate JavaScript, inspect history and read request logs, JS errors or page source. snapshot returns page text independently of the interaction viewport — whole pages up to one response and offset / rows windows with a Next offset for longer ones — so reading does not require scrolling. links and form page through large listings the same way. Screen captures are terminal-cell rasters; sessions are memory-only. The automation engine runs in a child process by default, so an engine crash does not take down the MCP server; later tool calls report the crash until the client restarts the server, and -process=single keeps the engine in the server process. Browser-style toolbar with back/forward/reload/home, history/help buttons, a config gear and a tab strip that stays visible even with one tab. Tabs: t / Ctrl-T opens a tab, W / Ctrl-W closes it, and a middle click on a tab closes it too. Closing a tab returns to the tab you used before it, while closing the last tab quits without the quit confirmation. Ctrl-N / Ctrl-P (or Alt-Right / Alt-Left , l / h ) move next/previous without wrapping, and 1 - 9 / Alt-1 - Alt-9 select one. New tabs from t / Ctrl-T , the + button and help are added at the end with an empty URL prompt when no target is given; the config gear opens a settings window over the current page instead of a new tab, and a link opened in a background tab ( F or middle click) is inserted directly after the current tab. Each tab keeps its own history, scroll, focus and overlays. Cookies follow normal domain rules across tabs, localStorage is shared by tabs on the same origin, and sessionStorage is per tab and origin. Graphics mode and zoom are browser-wide; the MEM readout is the active engine process, which in per-tab mode is the active tab's child rather than the browser total. Background tabs keep loading and update their title and history but do not draw; their timers and image work are paused so an idle tab stays at zero CPU. Closing a tab cancels its in-flight script requests first, so a page stuck in JavaScript cannot block the close. A crashed tab stays marked Crashed , shows the reason in a scrollable content view, and can be closed without stopping the others; a tab whose engine stops responding is force-stopped and kept the same way. By default each tab's engine runs in its own child process, which also contains fatal runtime errors such as a stack overflow to that tab; -process=single keeps all tabs in one process (see architecture ). Keyboard and mouse navigation, including the kitty keyboard protocol in kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and foot, a standard Tab order through links and form controls (Space/Enter activate, arrows or h/j/k/l change selects, radios and ranges), / or Ctrl-F incremental find (including multi-word phrases) with highlighted matches (also in the console HTML tab), a draggable page scrollbar, a browser-wide history overlay (H or Ctrl-H; selecting an entry opens it in the current tab, while back/forward stay per tab and keep each page's last scroll position), scrolling, focus, link hover targets and CSS/JS hover menus, text selection ( y copies selected text, or the current URL when nothing is selected), fixed-overlay hiding ( X ), and a ! JavaScript console. Rough edges Modern JavaScript apps (SPAs, dashboards, Google search and similar) may show a partial page, a blank page or their no-JavaScript fallback. ES module scripts run, including relative imports, dynamic import() , import.meta.url and bare specifiers resolved by the page's import map, and custom elements are constructed, but the rest of the JavaScript API surface is still a small subset. Reader mode is heuristic: it can pick too little or too much content on unusual layouts, shows a snapshot rather than live updates, and removes images, forms and other interactive controls. Dark mode keeps images in their original colors and approximates some author color schemes. Anti-bot and firewall checks (Cloudflare, CAPTCHA, DataDome/PerimeterX, Imperva/Sucuri, access-denied pages) are generally not usable. A “Site blocked” page is shown instead. No video/audio playback, WebGL, WebAssembly, workers, WebSockets, iframes, Shadow DOM, custom element lifecycle callbacks, real observers or full History API. Basic CSS grid rows and horizontal grid-item alignment are supported, along with position:relative , position:absolute and position:fixed , left/right floats with line wrapping and clear , and overflow clipping for bounded boxes. position:sticky with bottom / left / right , CSS variables, animations, transitions, non-text generated content, background gradients and repeating background tiles are unsupported or approximated. SVG support is partial: paths, shapes, gradients, transforms, clipPath , fill-rule and <use> of paths/groups work, but text inside SVG, <use> resolving to <symbol> sprites, filters, masks, CSS-in-SVG and animation are not rendered. Animated WebP is not supported. Oversized images are capped at 40% of the viewport width and 16 rows, preserving their aspect ratio. Images with a definite CSS box (both width and height) use object-fit / object-position and fill or crop into that box instead of hitting those caps. No automatic downloads; a download link opens a popup with a copyable curl command without fetching it, except a .pdf download, which opens in the text viewer ( -no-pdf restores the popup for PDFs), and multipart/file uploads are not implemented (clicking a file input shows an unsupported notice). Selected page text copies with y through OSC 52 (terminal support required), and the URL can be selected with the terminal. The console request list is shared across tabs, so a new document load in one tab clears it for the others; JavaScript logs, history and scroll stay per tab. Very large pages, stylesheets and images are capped to keep memory and CPU bounded. Style and layout also run under a time budget, so a pathological page is shown partially with a truncation notice instead of freezing the browser. Pages whose timers update the DOM are redrawn a few times per second rather than continuously, and an idle page with no pending timers does not repaint at all. Using command line options Start reading Wikipedia in dark mode: mob3 -dark -reader 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vim_(text_editor)' See all available options with -h flag. Every flag and default is listed in the command-line reference . Capture a PNG screenshot: mob3 -png -width 120 bbc.com/news > bbc.png Automation (MCP) mob3 -mcp runs a headless browser as a Model Context Protocol server over stdin/stdout. It accepts an optional starting URL and fixed -width : { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/path/to/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Tools: navigate , snapshot , links , form , fill , select , toggle , submit , click (link number, CSS selector or x / y ), type , press , wait , wait_for , scroll , back , forward , reload , evaluate , history , screenshot , requests , logs and source . snapshot returns the page text, paged with offset / rows for long pages; links and form page through the full link and control lists with selectors. screenshot returns a PNG of the terminal-cell raster, not a browser pixel capture. The server is local and unauthenticated, sessions and storage are memory-only, and nothing is written to disk. Do not expose it as a network service. See the full MCP reference for the protocol, snapshot format, tool details, and setup instructions for OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop. Development Build from source and start browsing: make ./bin/mob3 https://example.com Requires Go 1.27 or newer. Interactive mode runs on Linux and macOS terminals; Windows users can run it under WSL. When stdin/stdout are not a terminal, MOB3 automatically falls back to dump mode. Press ? inside the app for keys, mouse and forms help. Run mob3 -h for the full list of command-line flags. mob3 -version prints the embedded version. Contributing Contributions are welcome. Every pull request must include the broad license-grant CLA statement described in CONTRIBUTING.md ; it keeps MOB3 available under both AGPL-3.0-only and a commercial license. The PR workflow checks the statement. The CI workflow runs formatting, vet and tests, and a separate nightly workflow runs a vulnerability scan. License MOB3 is licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3 only (AGPL-3.0-only). Copyright (c) 2026 Milos Stojanovic. See LICENSE . A separate commercial license is available on request for use without the AGPL's source-disclosure requirements. MOB3 is provided “as is”, without warranty of any kind. See the warning at the top: this is not a security boundary. Third-party module licenses are listed in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES .","headings":[{"id":"documentation","text":"Documentation"},{"id":"try-it-with-docker","text":"Try it with Docker:"},{"id":"download-and-install","text":"Download and install"},{"id":"screenshots","text":"Screenshots"},{"id":"show-your-support","text":"Show your support"},{"id":"what-to-expect","text":"What to expect"},{"id":"using-command-line-options","text":"Using command line options"},{"id":"automation-mcp","text":"Automation (MCP)"},{"id":"development","text":"Development"},{"id":"contributing","text":"Contributing"},{"id":"license","text":"License"}]},{"path":"docs/architecture.html","title":"Architecture — MOB3 documentation","text":"Architecture This page is the map: the process model, the package boundaries, the rendering and JavaScript pipelines, and the tradeoffs behind them. It is deliberately an overview, not a file-by-file reference. Design goals One static binary. No cgo, no runtime service, no helper binary. The same executable is the browser, the per-tab worker and the MCP server. A terminal cell is the layout unit. Pages are laid out in columns and rows and painted as ANSI, not as a pixel surface. Memory-only operation. History, cookies, storage, caches and logs live in memory. Closing MOB3 saves nothing. Idle stays idle. No polling, prefetching or perpetual repaint loops. A background tab pauses its timers and image work. Fault isolation where it is cheap. Interactive tabs and MCP get their engine in a child process by default, so a fatal engine error does not take down the front end. This is not a security sandbox. Capability per unit of resource. Prefer the inexpensive approximation over the exact but expensive implementation: text PDFs instead of rasterized pages, block backgrounds instead of inline ones, half-block images instead of sixel. Text first. HTML, CSS, forms, links, find, selection, reader mode and JavaScript matter more than pixel-perfect rendering. The process tree -process=per-tab is the default. The parent owns the terminal and the network; each interactive tab and each MCP automation session gets its own engine child. mob3 parent process │ terminal, input, tabs, chrome, history │ host: network, TLS, cookies, cache, settings │ storage broker, history-entry IDs │ ├── tab 1 engine child │ DOM, JavaScript VM, layout, frames, PDF ├── tab 2 engine child │ DOM, JavaScript VM, layout, frames, PDF └── -mcp mode instead uses one automation engine child (no tabs) DOM, JavaScript VM, layout, frames, PDF -process=single keeps everything in one Go process: one mob3 process terminal, input, tabs, chrome, history host: network, TLS, cookies, cache, settings tab engines + MCP engine: DOM, JavaScript VM, layout, frames, PDF one -mem-limit, one address space Dump and PNG modes stay in-process in both modes. They are one-shot jobs and the terminal is never put into raw mode, so a process boundary buys little there. How a tab starts its engine A new tab does not start a second browser binary that has to be installed or found. It re-executes the running binary: parent, on a new tab │ ├─ os.Executable() + os.Args[1:] same binary, same flags ├─ worker marker + JSON geometry tab id, size, zoom, graphics ▼ child process ├─ parses the normal CLI flags one option surface ├─ opens host and storage RPC over stdin/stdout ├─ builds one engine session └─ sends Ready ▼ parent ├─ waits for Ready bounded handshake ├─ sends Init and Navigate └─ receives frames, state and events The parent holds one end of two anonymous pipes. A small binary multiplexer carries page commands and events, host requests, storage requests and liveness pings over that pair, so no TCP port or platform-specific file descriptor is needed. The child never writes the terminal; it returns frames and data, and the front end remains the only terminal writer. The same worker serves MCP: the parent runs the JSON-RPC server on stdio and starts an automation engine child. -process=single replaces the child with the in-process transport for both tabs and MCP. Closing and failure A normal tab close asks the child to shut down, waits for a short grace period, then kills it if it does not exit. A wedged tab is force-discarded without waiting for its handler. Child stderr is captured with a bounded, sanitized head and tail and shown in the crashed tab; it is never written directly to the terminal. An engine crash is contained to that child. The tab stays in the strip marked Crashed , other tabs keep running, and an MCP server stays alive but reports the crash on later tool calls until the client restarts it. In single mode the engine shares the browser process, so the same fatal error takes the whole browser down; terminal cleanup may not run. Isolation and containment Each tab is one protocol session with its own page, layout, JavaScript runtime and back/forward history. The parent shares the host, cookies, cache, settings and origin storage, and receives each document's final URL by entry ID, so the address bar never trusts a URL supplied by the engine. A background tab keeps loading and updating its state, title and history, but it does not draw, run JavaScript timers or stream deferred images. This is logical origin isolation plus fault isolation, not a security boundary . Tabs still share the parent host, storage broker and trust model. A compromised engine can read across tabs, and there is no OS sandbox, site isolation or Spectre mitigation. -process=single puts every tab back in one address space. Package boundaries The source is split so that the network, the terminal and the page engine do not reach into each other: Package Owns internal/protocol Wire types, binary codec, stream multiplexer, host and storage RPC, transports, limits. Standard library only; it never imports another project package. internal/engine HTML parsing, CSS, layout, painting, DOM, JavaScript, PDF extraction, document loading, find, selection, hints and reader mode. It reaches the network only through a host port. internal/host Sockets, TLS, DNS, proxies, cookies, the resource cache, settings and the request log. internal/frontend The terminal, input decoding, chrome, ANSI encoding, native images, dialogs, tabs and history. internal/app Wiring: flags, mode dispatch, process launch and the interactive loop. internal/textedit , internal/textwidth , internal/movekeys Pure line editing, terminal-cell arithmetic and viewport movement. The dependency direction is enforced by a test: the engine never imports the front end, host or net/http ; the front end never names an engine type; and the protocol package stays independent. Outside the standard library, the engine's only production imports are the approved vendored modules. That boundary is also what makes the process transport possible. Everything crossing a tab boundary is data: frames, events, placements, clipboard actions and host calls. No DOM node, image.Image , certificate pool or JavaScript value crosses it. Rendering HTML bytes → parse and build the document → selectors, cascade, computed styles → block and inline layout in terminal cells → paint into frames → front end encodes ANSI and writes the terminal The engine owns text measurement, line breaking, tables, floats, flex rows, positioning, backgrounds, borders and the text cursor. The front end owns the terminal encoding and presents only the active tab. Scaled text and native images are capabilities negotiated with the terminal. Optional images use kitty or iTerm2 native graphics first, then colored half-block art; there is no sixel. PDFs are read by an in-repo text extractor and shown as text pages, not rasterized. Images are off by default. JavaScript Page scripts run in a sobek runtime, one VM per top-level frame. Classic scripts run in document order, ES modules and import maps are supported, and external scripts require a JavaScript MIME type. The API surface is a practical subset: DOM queries and mutation, events, forms, timers, fetch /XHR through the host broker, in-memory storage, cookies and common compatibility shims. There is no WebGL, no worker execution, no real canvas pixels, no live MutationObserver , and no real iframe browsing context. A liveness watchdog can interrupt a long-running script; a native regular expression that cannot be interrupted is a documented limit. Networking and storage The host is the network boundary. The engine never opens a socket: document, CSS, script, image and form requests go through a host port, locally or over RPC. TLS, redirects, cookies, proxies and the resource cache live on the host side; the engine classifies the bytes and headers it receives. The parent also owns the storage broker: localStorage is shared by origin across tabs and sessionStorage is per tab and origin. The engine keeps a memory-only page cache for back/forward restoration. MOB3 writes no application files. Documents come from the network or about: pages; file: URLs are rejected, and data: is only an inline subresource. The only filesystem access is what the Go TLS, DNS and timezone libraries do, plus the self-exec used for worker processes. The host applies a local-network guard for subresources, upgrades or blocks mixed content and reports blocked requests in the console. This is not a sandbox, and top-level navigation is not blocked. See Security and privacy for the full model. Memory and efficiency -mem-limit derives the soft heap limit and the JS (25%), image (12%), SVG (3%), resource-cache (3%) and history-cache (12%) budgets. In single mode one BudgetManager divides those pools across tabs; in per-tab mode each engine child has its own process and its own limit, so the browser total is not capped by one number. Dump and PNG use the parent process. The design avoids work that does not produce a visible result: no prefetching, no polling, no background render loop, no automatic disk cache, and no drawing for background tabs. Long pages and unbounded inputs are capped; the dump path truncates by rows, the protocol has message and image limits, and the MCP tools page large results instead of returning them without bound. Tradeoffs Decision What it costs A separate engine process per tab Extra process memory and startup work; -mem-limit is per process in this mode. A text-first CSS and JavaScript subset Modern JavaScript-heavy apps may render partially or not at all. Terminal-cell layout Approximate CSS; not a pixel-perfect browser. Memory-only state Nothing survives a close, by design. Images off by default The fastest configuration is also the one without pictures. Text PDFs Scanned pages, figures and annotations are not rendered. No sandbox or site isolation The architecture is not a security boundary. Report-only MCP crash recovery The server survives an engine crash but does not respawn it. Further reading Command line for every flag and its limits. Security and privacy for the trust model and network policy. Automation with MCP for the headless surface. Development for builds, tests and release steps. The repository also carries file-by-file ownership notes for contributors: docs/agents/architecture.md .","headings":[{"id":"design-goals","text":"Design goals"},{"id":"the-process-tree","text":"The process tree"},{"id":"isolation-and-containment","text":"Isolation and containment"},{"id":"package-boundaries","text":"Package boundaries"},{"id":"rendering","text":"Rendering"},{"id":"javascript","text":"JavaScript"},{"id":"networking-and-storage","text":"Networking and storage"},{"id":"memory-and-efficiency","text":"Memory and efficiency"},{"id":"tradeoffs","text":"Tradeoffs"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading"}]},{"path":"docs/automation.html","title":"Automation with MCP — MOB3 documentation","text":"Automation with MCP mob3 -mcp runs MOB3 headlessly as a Model Context Protocol server over stdin/stdout. It exposes the browser to scripts and AI agents through a small set of text-oriented tools. mob3 -mcp mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home mob3 -mcp -width 120 https://en.wikipedia.org/ Optional starting URL; otherwise about:home . -width controls wrapping and coordinates. The interaction viewport is 30 rows by default; -height N sets it explicitly (about N-3 page rows after chrome). snapshot returns page text independently of that viewport, in offset / rows windows with a Next offset for long pages; the viewport still controls screenshots, scrolling and click coordinates. Images are disabled. -no-js , -layout-budget , -reader , -dark and search settings are honored. History, cookies and storage are in memory only. The automation engine runs in a child process by default, so an engine crash does not take down the MCP server or its stdio transport. The in-flight tool call and later calls return an engine crashed error; the engine is not respawned, so restart the server to recover. -process=single keeps the engine in the server process, where a fatal error also ends the server. The process model is described in architecture . -mem-limit applies to the engine child in per-tab mode and to the single server process with -process=single . Client configuration MOB3 speaks MCP over stdio, so any MCP client that can start a local command can use it. The examples below assume mob3 is installed at /usr/local/bin/mob3 ; replace that path with the output of command -v mob3 on your machine (for example /opt/homebrew/bin/mob3 on Apple Silicon). All clients accept the same server arguments. -width controls wrapping and coordinates, and the optional starting URL defaults to about:home : /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home OpenCode Add MOB3 as a global local server: opencode mcp add mob3 --global -- /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home opencode mcp list Or add it by hand to ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json (use opencode.json in a project for project-local access): { \"$schema\": \"https://opencode.ai/config.json\", \"mcp\": { \"servers\": { \"mob3\": { \"type\": \"local\", \"command\": [\"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } } OpenCode reloads configuration automatically; opencode mcp list shows the connection state. Claude Code Add MOB3 at user scope so it is available in every project: claude mcp add --transport stdio --scope user mob3 -- \\ /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home claude mcp list For a project-scoped server, put the equivalent entry in .mcp.json at the project root: { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Cursor Create .cursor/mcp.json in the project, or ~/.cursor/mcp.json for a global server: { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Reload the MCP servers from Cursor's settings after editing the file. Claude Desktop Add the server to Claude Desktop's configuration file ( claude_desktop_config.json ): { \"mcpServers\": { \"mob3\": { \"command\": \"/usr/local/bin/mob3\", \"args\": [\"-mcp\", \"-width\", \"100\", \"about:home\"] } } } Restart Claude Desktop after changing the file. Other clients Clients that use the common mcpServers shape can use the same entry shown for Cursor. The only required fields are command and args ; the server needs no environment variables, URL or API key. MCP communication is stdio-only. Stdout carries protocol messages only; diagnostics go to stderr. Do not expose the process as a network service; there is no authentication. Protocol Messages are newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0. {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\"}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"notifications/initialized\",\"params\":{}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"tools/list\",\"params\":{}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":3,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"navigate\",\"arguments\":{\"url\":\"https://example.com/\"}}} Supported methods: initialize — returns capabilities.tools , serverInfo and short usage instructions. ping — empty result. tools/list — tool names, descriptions and JSON schemas. tools/call — runs one tool. notifications/initialized and other notifications — acknowledged by silence. Tool failures are returned as successful JSON-RPC responses whose content explains the error and whose isError field is true . Protocol errors use standard JSON-RPC error codes. You can test the server without an MCP client: printf '%s\\n' \\ '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\"}}' \\ '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"navigate\",\"arguments\":{\"url\":\"https://example.com/\"}}}' \\ | ./bin/mob3 -mcp Debugging in the running browser The interactive browser's ! console has a Tools tab for read-only inspection of the page you are looking at. Its commands mirror the inspection tools below: help , form [selector] , links [selector] , requests [limit] , logs [limit] , source [selector] , snapshot , history and clear . Results scroll in the tab and keep the newest 500 lines, help lists the arguments and selector examples, and the scrollback survives page loads. clear (or cls ) empties the Tools scrollback; the JS logs input accepts the same command to clear the JavaScript log. A [selector] is an optional CSS selector such as #content , .card , form#search , input[name=q] or main a ; omit it to inspect all forms/links or the full page source. Navigation, form editing ( fill / select / toggle / submit ), wait / wait_for and screenshot are available only through mob3 -mcp : the interactive event loop owns navigation results, so those commands cannot run synchronously from inside it. Tools Tool Arguments Returns navigate {url} Snapshot after loading snapshot {offset?, rows?} Page text window, row range and next offset links {selector?, offset?, limit?} Links page with index, text, href, selector and next offset screenshot {scale} PNG image of the current page viewport click {link} or {text} or {selector} or {x,y} Snapshot after the click form {selector?, offset?, limit?} Controls page with CSS selectors, values and next offset fill {selector,value} or {fields} filled N field(s) select {selector,value} or {values} or {text} or {index} selected … toggle {selector,checked?} … is checked / … is unchecked submit {selector?} Snapshot after navigation wait {ms} waited <ms>ms wait_for {selector?} and/or {text?} , {timeout_ms?} found … after <ms> type {text} typed press {key} pressed <key> scroll {lines} or {pages} or {top} or {bottom} Scroll percentage back {} Snapshot forward {} Snapshot reload {} Snapshot evaluate {expression} JavaScript result as text history {} Session history, most recent first requests {limit?} Current page's request log and cache usage logs {limit?} JavaScript console messages and errors source {selector?} Raw decoded HTML, or one element's serialized HTML navigate Resolves input like the URL prompt: URLs, about: pages, or a search query through -search . Local files are not supported; file: URLs are rejected. Waits for the page, meta refreshes and scripted redirects. snapshot Returns the page as plain text starting at the first layout row, not just the interaction viewport. A page that fits one response is returned whole; a longer page returns one window plus a Next offset. Pass offset and optional rows to read the rest without scrolling: {\"offset\": 1240, \"rows\": 200} The header reports the page height, the returned layout-row range and the next offset; Next is absent on the last window: URL: https://example.com/ Title: Example Domain Page: 100x1400 Rows: 0-230 of 1400 Next: 230 Viewport: 100x27 Scroll: 0% Example Domain ... offset is a zero-based layout row. rows caps the window; omit it (or pass 0) to fill one response. Snapshot is text only and does not depend on scroll; use links and form for link and control lists, selectors and values. links {} {\"selector\":\"main a\"} {\"offset\":200,\"limit\":100} Lists every rendered anchor in the document, including links outside the current viewport, with the [n] index that click accepts: Links: 2 Showing: 1-2 of 2 [1] Docs -> https://example.com/docs selector=\"a[href=\\\"/docs\\\"]\" [2] Sign in -> https://example.com/login selector=\"#nav a:nth-child(2)\" Empty anchors, hidden anchors and anchors with no rendered content are omitted. selector filters which anchors are listed (it matches the anchor, not its containers) before paging. A response holds up to 2,000 links or about 50,000 characters; Showing reports the range and Next is the 0-based offset for the following call. Most pages fit in one response. form {} {\"selector\":\"form#search\"} {\"offset\":200,\"limit\":100} Lists every form and its controls, including fields that are outside the current viewport. Each control shows its tag, name, type, current value or checked state, flags ( required , readonly , disabled ) and a stable CSS selector for the other form tools: Forms: 1 Controls: 3 Showing: 1-3 of 3 [1] form method=\"GET\" action=\"/find\" selector=\"#search\" - input name=\"q\" type=\"text\" value=\"old\" selector=\"#search input[name=\\\"q\\\"]\" - select name=\"lang\" value=\"fr\" options=[\"en\" \"fr\"] selector=\"select[name=\\\"lang\\\"]\" - input name=\"safe\" type=\"checkbox\" state=checked value=\"on\" selector=\"#search input[name=\\\"safe\\\"]\" Hidden inputs are omitted because they cannot be edited. Controls outside any form are listed separately. Password input values are never printed: a non-empty password shows value=\"[redacted]\" , an empty one shows value=\"\" . Selectors prefer an #id , then a form-id name pair, then an nth-child path. Pass a form or control selector to list just that form. A response holds up to 2,000 controls or about 50,000 characters; Showing reports the range and Next is the 0-based offset for the following call. A page that starts inside a form repeats that form's header. fill {\"selector\":\"#search input[name=q]\",\"value\":\"terminal browsers\"} {\"fields\":{\"#search input[name=q]\":\"terminal browsers\",\"textarea[name=notes]\":\"a note\"}} fill sets text-like inputs and textareas, replacing any existing value; an empty string clears the field. It accepts one selector/value pair or a fields map of selector to value. Values honor maxlength , and range inputs accept a number. Checkboxes, radios and selects are rejected: use toggle and select . fill never submits and never requires focus, so it also works on off-screen fields. Each change fires input and change . select {\"selector\":\"select[name=lang]\",\"value\":\"fr\"} {\"selector\":\"select[name=tags]\",\"values\":[\"a\",\"b\"]} {\"selector\":\"select[name=lang]\",\"text\":\"French\"} {\"selector\":\"select[name=lang]\",\"index\":1} Chooses by option value, visible label, or zero-based option index. Pass values for a <select multiple> to replace the selected set. A missing value or an out-of-range index is a tool error that lists the available options. toggle {\"selector\":\"input[name=remember]\"} {\"selector\":\"input[name=remember]\",\"checked\":true} {\"selector\":\"option[value=b]\",\"checked\":true} Checks or unchecks a checkbox or radio. Omit checked to flip the current state. Selecting a radio unchecks the other radios in its group, including groups outside a form. Multi-select options can also be toggled this way. Use select to choose a single-select option or replace a whole multiple selection. submit {} {\"selector\":\"form#search\"} Submits a form: runs the supported constraint validation, fires the form's submit handler, builds the GET query or urlencoded POST body, navigates, and returns a snapshot. Without a selector it uses the focused control's form, or the page's only form. Validation failures, invalid actions and preventDefault() are returned as tool errors (validation also focuses the first invalid field), so the page does not change on failure. wait and wait_for {\"ms\":500} {\"selector\":\"#results\",\"timeout_ms\":5000} {\"text\":\"No results found\",\"timeout_ms\":2000} {\"selector\":\".item\",\"text\":\"price\",\"timeout_ms\":5000} JavaScript timers do not run on their own between tool calls. wait advances the page for a number of milliseconds (default 100, maximum 10000), running due timers, applying the DOM rebuilds they cause and waiting for navigations they start. wait_for polls until a CSS selector matches an element and/or the given text appears in the rendered page. Text matching is case-insensitive, ignores markup and scripts, and sees the laid-out document rather than just the viewport. It returns found … after <ms> or fails with a timeout error; the default timeout is 5000ms and the maximum is 30000ms. When both selector and text are supplied, both must match. Both tools return a short confirmation rather than a snapshot; call snapshot afterwards when you need the page contents. screenshot {\"scale\":1} Returns an MCP image/png content block of the current page viewport, plus a short text line with the URL. scale 1 uses 8×16 pixel cells; scale 2 doubles the image. This is a terminal-cell raster, not a browser pixel screenshot: Go Mono glyphs are used, CJK/emoji glyphs may be missing, and MCP disables image downloads so image regions show alt text. click {\"link\":1} {\"text\":\"Sign in\"} {\"selector\":\"input[name=q]\"} {\"x\":4,\"y\":12} link uses a links link number, including off-screen links. text clicks the most specific rendered element whose visible text matches, case-insensitively. Exact matches are preferred, then links and buttons over containers. It also matches submit-button value text and aria-label / alt fallbacks. selector uses a CSS selector. Links, inputs, checkboxes and buttons are clicked through their rendered cell, so normal browser click behaviour runs. A selector click scrolls an off-screen element into view first. x / y are zero-based page-viewport cells. Pass exactly one of the four targets. type and press type requires a focused input, textarea or the URL prompt. Click a field first. A newline presses Enter; a tab presses Tab. press accepts Enter , Tab , Escape , Backspace , Delete , arrows, Home , End , PageUp , PageDown and Space , plus single characters. evaluate Runs JavaScript in the page realm through sobek: {\"expression\":\"document.title\"} {\"expression\":\"JSON.stringify([...document.querySelectorAll('h2')].map(h => h.textContent))\"} Objects stringify as [object Object] ; use JSON.stringify for structured results. -no-js disables evaluation. requests , logs and source {\"limit\":50} {\"limit\":20} {\"selector\":\"main article\"} These expose the same diagnostics as the in-browser ! console: requests lists the current page's in-memory request log — status, method, size, kind ( doc , css , js , img , fetch , xhr ) and URL — followed by cache bytes used. limit keeps the newest entries (default 100; 0 shows all, up to the 512-entry cap). logs returns JavaScript console messages and uncaught errors in order, with · error in the header when the page has a script error. limit keeps the newest entries (default 200; 0 shows all retained entries). Logs and the error flag reset when a new page is applied and are lost on failed loads. source returns the raw decoded HTML that was fetched, before scripts ran. With selector , it returns the serialized HTML of that element instead. Terminal control characters are removed. Large sources may be clipped by the MCP transport. history Lists session history, most recent first; * marks the current entry. Typical agent loop navigate a page. snapshot to read the page text; use links for link numbers and selectors, including links below the fold. form to get every control's selector and current value. fill , select and toggle to set fields without focusing or scrolling. submit to send the form, then read the returned snapshot. Use wait_for after clicks and submissions that load content with timers, then snapshot when ready. Use click / type / press for special widgets, evaluate for DOM extraction, scroll for screenshots or coordinate clicks, and back / history to move. When a page loads wrong, check requests for blocked or missing resources, logs for script errors, and source for the markup that was fetched. Limitations Terminal-cell rasters. screenshot returns a PNG of the terminal-cell raster, not a browser pixel capture: MOB3 has no font rasterizer, so glyphs are monospaced and some CJK/emoji glyphs may be missing. One page per server. The headless session has a single page and no tab UI; tabs are an interactive-mode feature. Iframes are inert stubs, so there is no child browsing context. Sized viewport. The virtual viewport is 30 rows by default; -width and -height set it. JavaScript is a subset. ES modules run, but workers, service workers and many modern APIs are unavailable. Forms. fill , select , toggle and submit handle text fields, textareas, checkboxes, radios and selects. File uploads and multipart forms are not supported, and framework widgets that rerender from their own state may overwrite a value set directly. Timers advance only during loads and waits. JavaScript timers run while a page loads and during wait / wait_for ; a timer that fires later needs one of those tools before its content appears. Unrelated tool calls do not pump timers. Memory only. Closing the server discards history, cookies and storage. Local, unauthenticated server. Use it only over a trusted local stdio channel. See Security and privacy for the broader security model.","headings":[{"id":"client-configuration","text":"Client configuration"},{"id":"protocol","text":"Protocol"},{"id":"debugging-in-the-running-browser","text":"Debugging in the running browser"},{"id":"tools","text":"Tools"},{"id":"typical-agent-loop","text":"Typical agent loop"},{"id":"limitations","text":"Limitations"}]},{"path":"docs/cli.html","title":"Command line — MOB3 documentation","text":"Command line mob3 [flags] [url] With no URL, MOB3 opens the -home target ( about:home by default). Input that looks like a search query instead of a URL uses the -search fallback. Local file pages are not supported: file: URLs are rejected and a bare path is treated as a URL or search query, never read from disk. Interactive mode starts only when stdin and stdout are TTYs. Otherwise MOB3 falls back to dump mode and prints the rendered page. Flags Flag Default Meaning -dump off Render the page to stdout and exit -png off Write a PNG screenshot of the dump to stdout; implies -dump . PDFs render one page (page 1 unless -pdf-page chooses another) -width int terminal width or 100 Layout width in columns -height int 0 Layout viewport height and output limit; 0 uses the full page for dump/PNG and 30 rows for MCP; MCP snapshots are independent of the viewport and page with offset / rows -pdf-page string auto PDF page for dump/PNG: auto , all , or a page number. -png defaults to page 1; a dump defaults to all pages -graphics string none Image mode: none , auto , kitty , iterm , blocks -g string none Alias for -graphics -no-js off Disable page script fetching and execution -no-pdf off Disable the built-in PDF text viewer and show the download prompt instead -reader off Start in reader mode -dark off Start in dark mode -ignore-blocks off Load anti-bot/CAPTCHA pages instead of the blocked page -home string about:home Startup and Home-button target -mem-limit int 0 Soft memory limit per process in MB; in single mode one process is shared by all tabs and the MCP engine, while -process=per-tab gives each tab child and each MCP engine child its own limit; derives the JS (25%), image (12%), SVG (3%), resource-cache (3%) and history-cache (12%) budgets; 0 leaves it unlimited and honors GOMEMLIMIT -layout-budget string standard Layout limits: low , standard , high , none -zoom string 100% Layout zoom percent from 25 to 200; % is optional and + / - step the presets -text-scale string auto Kitty scaled text: auto or off -color off Force ANSI colors when dumping -no-color off Disable ANSI colors in dump mode -ua string MOB3/1.0 (+terminal; +https://mob3.net) Custom User-Agent -search string DuckDuckGo HTML endpoint Search URL template with %s ; empty disables the fallback -certs string system TLS trust store: system or embedded -process string per-tab Engine placement: per-tab (one child engine process per interactive tab or MCP session) or single (one process; lowest memory, debugging and constrained hosts) -mcp off Run the MCP automation server on stdin/stdout -version Print the version and exit -h , -help Print flags and exit Run mob3 -h for the exact defaults in your build. Process placement -process=per-tab (the default) runs each interactive tab and an MCP automation session in its own engine child; -process=single keeps everything in one process. See the process model for the spawn handshake, what stays in the parent, crash containment and the memory tradeoff. Proxy MOB3 has no -proxy flag or proxy settings UI, but the host transport follows Go's standard environment proxy variables: HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.example:3128 \\ HTTP_PROXY=http://proxy.example:3128 \\ NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1 \\ bin/mob3 The variables are read by http.ProxyFromEnvironment when the process starts. HTTP_PROXY and HTTPS_PROXY cover the corresponding request schemes; NO_PROXY excludes hosts. ALL_PROXY is not used by Go's environment proxy lookup. SOCKS5 is supported by Go's HTTP transport, so an SSH tunnel works too: ssh -N -D 1080 user@proxy.example HTTPS_PROXY=socks5://127.0.0.1:1080 \\ HTTP_PROXY=socks5://127.0.0.1:1080 \\ NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1 \\ bin/mob3 The proxy is used by the parent host process, which owns all network requests. It applies to interactive, dump, PNG and MCP modes. In -process=per-tab , the engine children still send host RPC to the parent, so the proxy applies there too. Proxy URLs may contain credentials ( http://user:pass@host:port ). Environment variables are visible to child processes and to ps e -style inspection on some systems, so prefer an SSH tunnel or a local authenticated proxy when the credentials matter. This is best-effort networking, not a security boundary: the proxy sees HTTP traffic and destination hostnames, HTTPS remains end-to-end encrypted unless the proxy performs MITM, and the local-network guard cannot inspect the target the proxy resolves. Images and graphics -graphics=none is the default: no external image downloads and no inline SVG rasterization. When enabled, native terminal graphics are preferred: kitty graphics protocol iTerm2 inline images colored half-block art ( ▀ / ▄ ) Auto-detection uses environment variables. Under tmux, screen or zellij it falls back to half-block art because the multiplexer usually consumes graphics escapes unless passthrough is configured. -g is an alias for -graphics . Dump mode always uses half-block art for images that were loaded. An image with page text painted over it, such as a caption or a tab strip on a hero photo, is also drawn as half-block art: native placements composite above the text grid and would hide that text. Images stream in after the page text and appear progressively. A batch that only affects images below the current viewport does not redraw the visible frame, so the page stays steady while the rest loads; content below is laid out when you scroll to it. Color and dump mode -dump prints the whole document height (capped at 20,000 rows) without browser chrome. Color is automatic on a TTY, forced by -color , and disabled by -no-color . Output uses 24-bit color, except on macOS Terminal.app where it falls back to the 256-color palette because that terminal does not support 24-bit SGR sequences. With -no-color , interactive output keeps bold and underline attributes (links stay underlined and link hints use reverse video); dumps stay plain text. mob3 -dump -width 120 page.html mob3 -dump -width 120 -no-color https://go.dev mob3 -dump -color https://example.com Page height -height N sets the layout viewport to N rows and limits dump/PNG output to N document rows. This affects position:fixed boxes and other height-dependent layout. -height 0 keeps the full document height in dump and PNG output, while -mcp uses 30 rows when no height is given. The interactive UI always uses the real terminal height. mob3 -dump -height 40 -width 120 page.html mob3 -png -height 40 -width 120 page.html > page.png mob3 -mcp -width 100 -height 60 PNG screenshots -png renders the dump as a PNG stream on stdout. It implies -dump , is capped at 2,000 rows, and uses an 8×16 pixel cell with Go Mono glyphs: mob3 -png -width 120 bbc.com/news > bbc.png mob3 -png -g blocks -width 120 bbc.com/news > bbc-images.png Image cells render as colored half-block art. The renderer is text-oriented: CJK and emoji glyphs are missing from the bundled Go Mono font, and there is no browser chrome or true pixel screenshot. Layout budget -layout-budget bundles the style/layout deadline and fragment/document-row caps: Preset Deadline Fragments Rows low 1 s 100,000 80,000 standard 2 s 250,000 200,000 high 5 s 1,000,000 1,000,000 none disabled disabled disabled none can freeze the UI on hostile or generated pages. The preset is editable in the config window; a zero individual limit disables that check. When a limit is hit, the page renders partially and the status says Page truncated (layout limit) . Page zoom -zoom 150 starts at 150% (values 25 to 200, % optional). In the interactive browser + and - step through 25, 50, 67, 80, 100, 125, 150, 175 and 200%, and 0 returns to 100%. Zoom changes how many CSS pixels one terminal cell represents, not the text size: body text keeps one cell per character and its normal line height, while fixed pixel widths, spacing, borders, images and kitty scaled large headings grow or shrink. The CSS viewport follows the ratio, so responsive pages reflow. It applies to dump, PNG and MCP runs, is editable in the config window, and is never persisted. In the interactive browser the URL row always shows the current level as a clickable badge at its far right; clicking it opens a window for choosing a preset with the mouse or arrow keys and Enter . TLS certificate store -certs chooses where HTTPS trust comes from: Value Trust store system (default) The operating system's root certificates, read by Go when a TLS connection needs them; if the system has no roots at all, the embedded bundle is used automatically embedded The Mozilla NSS root set, compiled into the binary from golang.org/x/crypto/x509roots/fallback The embedded bundle is the official one maintained by the Go project: it is parsed from Mozilla's NSS trust store and keeps Mozilla's distrust-after dates, so a constrained root stops validating certificates issued after that date. It is refreshed by bumping the module and re-vendoring, not by editing a data file: go get golang.org/x/crypto/x509roots/fallback@latest go mod vendor The chosen store replaces the operating system store instead of adding to it, so the same roots are trusted on every machine. This matters on an operating system the vendor no longer updates: its CA package may be missing or years out of date, and -certs=embedded restores modern HTTPS without touching the rest of the system. The automatic fallback covers only a system with no roots; an old but present CA package is not overridden, which is exactly when the flag is needed. The value can be switched between system and embedded for the session in the config window. It applies to every later request, and pooled connections made with the previous store are closed. Certificate files are not supported: the binary reads no local bundle. An unknown value at startup is an error and the run stops; a rejected value in the config window keeps the current store and shows the reason in the window. Trust-store choice is not a security boundary: revocation is not checked, and there is no certificate UI, pinning or warning flow beyond the connection error the request returns. JavaScript and cache limits Each page has an absolute heap ceiling above the heap at runtime creation. One sampler per active runtime checks it every 75 ms and interrupts the running call; a page that retains allocations cannot reset the budget on each callback. The ceiling is 25% of -mem-limit / GOMEMLIMIT (for example 256 MB at a 1 GB limit), or 256 MB when the process is unlimited; raise -mem-limit to raise it. Every script also runs under a 2-second CPU watchdog. There is no separate per-page flag and no way to disable the memory check while keeping JavaScript on. Each page's load-time scripts share an 8-second wall-clock budget and run at most 2,000 scripts; on either limit the running script is interrupted and the remaining ones are skipped with a console notice. Esc or Ctrl-C interrupts the script currently blocking the terminal and cancels its in-flight requests; closing the tab does the same. Decoded image bytes in flight and inline-SVG rasters are bounded by percentages of the same memory limit and by absolute fallbacks. -no-js disables both script fetching and execution; <noscript> content is then shown. Press J (or click the footer JavaScript label) to toggle this for the session and reload the current page. The resource cache and the back/forward history cache follow the same limit: 3% and 12% of -mem-limit / GOMEMLIMIT , with 32 MB and 128 MB fallbacks when the process is unlimited. The resource cache is a session-only LRU for CSS/JS and image response bodies plus decoded images; nothing is written to disk, and cache hits appear in the ! console. The history cache weighs suspended pages and evicts the least recently used ones. Home and search -home sets the page opened at startup and by the ⌂ toolbar button. The -search template receives the URL-escaped query in %s ; the default uses the server-rendered DuckDuckGo HTML endpoint. An empty value disables search fallback, so only URL-like input navigates. Automation -mcp starts the headless MCP server instead of the terminal UI: mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home See Automation with MCP for the client configuration, protocol and tools. about:config The ⚙ toolbar icon, or typing about:config , opens a settings window over the current page. It lists only the options that can change at runtime: JavaScript, the PDF viewer, blocklist overrides, the home page, the memory limit, the layout budget, zoom, text scaling, the user agent, the search URL and the certificate store. Startup and dump-only options are not shown, and nothing is persisted. Use ↑ / ↓ to choose a row, Enter or Space to toggle a checkbox or cycle a choice, Enter to edit a text value, and Esc to close. Toggles and choices apply immediately; a text value applies when the edit is committed. A rejected value keeps the current setting and shows the reason in the window. The window works over any page, including reader mode; a page link to about:config opens the same window.","headings":[{"id":"flags","text":"Flags"},{"id":"process-placement","text":"Process placement"},{"id":"proxy","text":"Proxy"},{"id":"images-and-graphics","text":"Images and graphics"},{"id":"color-and-dump-mode","text":"Color and dump mode"},{"id":"layout-budget","text":"Layout budget"},{"id":"page-zoom","text":"Page zoom"},{"id":"tls-certificate-store","text":"TLS certificate store"},{"id":"javascript-and-cache-limits","text":"JavaScript and cache limits"},{"id":"home-and-search","text":"Home and search"},{"id":"automation","text":"Automation"},{"id":"about-config","text":"about:config"}]},{"path":"docs/development.html","title":"Development — MOB3 documentation","text":"Development Requirements Go 1.27 or newer make no C toolchain: builds use CGO_ENABLED=0 dependencies are committed under vendor/ , so builds need no network Build and test make make test make fuzz go vet ./... gofmt -w <files> make writes a static bin/mob3 . make clean removes it. make vendor regenerates vendor/ after a dependency change and must be committed. make hooks points Git at .githooks/ ; its pre-commit hook rejects a staged file above 2 MB (the generated vendor/ tree is exempt), and CI runs .githooks/check-large-files --all over every tracked file so --no-verify cannot slip one through. Compress or split the file instead. Tests must not require network access. Use the existing fake loader and httptest -free helpers where possible. Add or update tests in the same change as behaviour. Efficiency target The product goal is to cover most of the features people actually use while using far less CPU and memory than a mainstream browser. Concretely, when a choice is ambiguous, prefer the cheap approximation over the exact but expensive implementation, keep idle pages asleep, cap unbounded work, and never trade a large steady-state footprint for a marginal feature. See the working agreement in AGENTS.md for the rule in force. Measure locally, before and after, whenever a change touches the draw loop, timers, layout, the style pass or the JavaScript runtime. Comparing wall time, peak RSS and idle CPU on the same machine and the same pages is what matters; do not record hardware- or corpus-specific figures in the docs, because they go stale and invite false comparisons. make ls -l bin/mob3 TIMEFORMAT='%3R s'; time (for i in $(seq 20); do bin/mob3 -version >/dev/null; done) /usr/bin/time -v bin/mob3 -dump -width 177 -no-color page.html >/dev/null Peak RSS in -dump includes Go GC headroom and is higher than a settled interactive session, so compare like with like. For settled interactive RSS and idle CPU, run the browser inside a pseudo terminal ( script -qec \"bin/mob3 FILE\" /dev/null ), take the mob3 PID from pgrep , and compare fields 14+15 of /proc/<pid>/stat (utime + stime) over a window: an idle page must not consume measurable CPU. Per-phase parse, layout and live-heap attribution comes from a temporary zz_*_test.go benchmark following the debugging recipe in docs/agents/debugging.md ; delete it afterwards. Documentation README.md is the short landing page: what MOB3 is, how to run it, and what to expect. docs/ holds long-form documentation. mob3 -h and the in-app about:help page are canonical for flags and keys. docs/cli.md and docs/keys.md must stay in sync. docs/automation.md documents the MCP tools. Tool names come from mcpToolDefinitions() in internal/app/mcp.go . AGENTS.md is the agent entry point; domain-specific engine, UI, network, build and JS decisions live under docs/agents/ and are not published by the site build. The GitHub Pages site is generated from README.md and docs/*.md by .github/pages/build.sh , using pinned marked . Build the site locally: .github/pages/build.sh /tmp/mob3-site The script writes index.html , docs/*.html , search-index.js , robots.txt , sitemap.xml , the favicon and social-preview files under assets/ , a CNAME for mob3.net and .nojekyll . Relative .md links are rewritten to .html for the site. Docs pages share a generated sidebar, on-page “On this page” contents list and a compact search box at the right edge of the header, with Docs, GitHub, Releases and Contact links, a light/dark theme toggle after the search and a mobile menu. On phones the search is dropped so the whole header stays on one line. Every page carries a canonical URL, description, Open Graph and Twitter card tags and JSON-LD structured data, so search results and link previews use the intended title, summary and 1200×630 image. The assets under .github/pages/assets/ and the README screenshots under docs/screenshots/ are committed; replace og-image.png when the hero screenshot changes. Repository layout Path Purpose cmd/mob3/ Executable entry point internal/engine/ HTML, CSS, layout, rendering, JS/DOM, PDF, images, loader, session, automation and dump internal/host/ Network, cookies, cache, certificates, settings and request log internal/frontend/ Terminal, input, chrome, ANSI/PNG and native images internal/app/ Flags, mode dispatch, wiring, MCP transport and dump output docs/ User documentation .github/pages/ GitHub Pages template and build script vendor/ Committed Go dependencies bin/ Local build output (ignored) Key engine files are listed in docs/agents/architecture.md . The MCP and automation split is: automation.go — headless browser commands and text snapshots mcp.go — MCP/JSON-RPC framing; delegates to Automation.RunCommand cli.go — flags, dump mode, interactive loop and -mcp startup Workflows CI runs gofmt, go vet and tests on pushes to main and pull requests. Vulnerability scan runs govulncheck nightly and on manual dispatch; it downloads the scanner and needs network access, while the normal build/test job stays vendored and offline. PR requires the exact CLA statement described in CONTRIBUTING.md . Release binaries builds seven targets on v* tags. Docker publishes the multi-arch image after a successful release. GitHub Pages rebuilds mob3.net from README.md and docs/ when they change. Contributing Read CONTRIBUTING.md before opening a pull request. Contributions require the broad license-grant CLA so the project can remain available under AGPL-3.0-only or a commercial license. Keep the working agreement in AGENTS.md and the engine decisions under docs/agents/ current whenever behaviour changes.","headings":[{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements"},{"id":"build-and-test","text":"Build and test"},{"id":"efficiency-target","text":"Efficiency target"},{"id":"documentation","text":"Documentation"},{"id":"repository-layout","text":"Repository layout"},{"id":"workflows","text":"Workflows"},{"id":"contributing","text":"Contributing"}]},{"path":"docs/index.html","title":"MOB3 documentation","text":"MOB3 documentation MOB3 is a small web browser for the terminal, written in Go. It has its own HTML parser, CSS cascade, layout engine, ANSI renderer and pure-Go JavaScript runtime. History, cookies, storage and caches live in memory only. The project aims to cover most of the features people actually use while using a fraction of the CPU and memory of a mainstream browser. See architecture for the process model, package boundaries and the tradeoffs behind the design. Warning: MOB3 is not a secure browser . It has no sandboxing, process or site isolation, CSP enforcement, certificate UI or security audit. Do not use it for passwords, accounts, payments or confidential data. Quick start Download an archive from GitHub Releases , or run the container: docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mob3net/mob3 -graphics blocks Build from source: make ./bin/mob3 https://example.com Press ? inside the app for the built-in help page, and run mob3 -h for the full flag list. What can you do with it? See use cases for developers and sysadmins, AI agents and MCP, everyday terminal users, and automation and integration. The short version: browse over SSH, test and inspect pages headlessly, scrape JavaScript-driven content, generate PNG screenshots, run browser checks in CI, and give AI agents a local web browser.","headings":[{"id":"quick-start","text":"Quick start"},{"id":"what-can-you-do-with-it","text":"What can you do with it?"}]},{"path":"docs/install.html","title":"Install — MOB3 documentation","text":"Install Release archives Download the latest archive from GitHub Releases . Each release contains: mob3_<tag>_linux_386.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_amd64.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_armv6.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_armv7.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_linux_arm64.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_darwin_amd64.tar.gz mob3_<tag>_darwin_arm64.tar.gz SHA256SUMS Every archive contains the static mob3 binary. Example: tar -xzf mob3_vX.Y.Z_linux_amd64.tar.gz sudo install -m 0755 mob3 /usr/local/bin/mob3 mob3 -version Windows users run the Linux binary under WSL. Interactive mode needs a real Linux or macOS terminal. Old or unsupported systems The binary is static, so it needs no runtime libraries. A system with no CA certificate package at all is handled automatically: the Mozilla NSS root bundle maintained by the Go project is compiled in and used when the platform has no roots. A system whose CA package is old but present should start with -certs=embedded to trust that same current bundle instead; it replaces the platform store for the session. See TLS certificate store . Docker docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mob3net/mob3 -graphics blocks The image is published for linux/386 , linux/amd64 , linux/arm64 , linux/arm/v6 and linux/arm/v7 . It runs as a non-root user and includes LICENSE and third-party notices. It is a terminal application: use -it to attach it to a TTY. The image installs no CA certificate package. HTTPS uses the Mozilla NSS bundle compiled into the binary, which Go's fallback trust applies when the container has no platform roots, so the trust set is the one that shipped with that release. Certificate files are not supported, so private roots require a build with the bundle embedded. MCP clients After installing mob3 , you can use it as a headless MCP server. OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop all start it as a local stdio server: mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home For OpenCode, one command registers it globally: opencode mcp add mob3 --global -- /usr/local/bin/mob3 -mcp -width 100 about:home See Automation with MCP for the OpenCode, Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop configuration details. Build from source Requirements: Go 1.27 or newer make no C toolchain: the build is CGO_ENABLED=0 and produces a static binary dependencies are vendored, so no network is needed to build make ./bin/mob3 https://example.com Useful targets: make # static binary in bin/ make test # go test -mod=vendor ./... make vendor # refresh vendor/ after dependency changes make docker # local container image make clean # remove bin/ Set a release version locally: make VERSION=vX.Y.Z ./bin/mob3 -version internal/app.Version defaults to dev ; release builds inject the Git tag through linker flags. The built binary is ignored by Git and make clean removes it. First run No configuration is written. The first run opens about:home unless a URL is supplied. Use -home URL to choose a different start page. Local file pages are not supported.","headings":[{"id":"release-archives","text":"Release archives"},{"id":"old-or-unsupported-systems","text":"Old or unsupported systems"},{"id":"docker","text":"Docker"},{"id":"mcp-clients","text":"MCP clients"},{"id":"build-from-source","text":"Build from source"},{"id":"first-run","text":"First run"}]},{"path":"docs/keys.html","title":"Keyboard and mouse — MOB3 documentation","text":"Keyboard and mouse Press ? inside MOB3 for the built-in about:help page, which is the canonical key, mouse and form reference for the running build. Page navigation Key Action j / k Scroll one line down / up Space / b Scroll one screen down / up d / u (or Ctrl-D / Ctrl-U ) Scroll half a screen down / up g / G Top / bottom of the page Tab / Shift-Tab Next / previous link or form control Enter Follow the focused link, submit the focused field, or insert a newline in a textarea Ctrl-O / [ Back Ctrl-I / ] Forward (Ctrl-I needs kitty key reporting; legacy terminals send Tab, so ] is the portable key) r / Ctrl-R Reload R Toggle reader mode D Toggle dark mode J Toggle JavaScript and reload the page without or with scripts i Cycle graphics modes in place: full graphics, blocks, none (no reload) + / - Zoom the page in / out (25% to 200%). Fixed pixel layout, spacing, images and large kitty headings grow; body text keeps its size and line height 0 Reset zoom to 100% Z Open the zoom window to choose a preset with the mouse, arrow keys, mouse wheel or pointer hover X Open the view options window (Fixed elements, Sticky elements, Background images); Space / Enter toggles the selected switch, and the mouse wheel or pointer hover moves the highlight o / Ctrl-L Open the URL prompt / , Ctrl-F Find in the page, or in the console HTML tab n / N Next / previous find match f Highlight letters in the visible links and follow one by typing them F Open the chosen link in a background tab after the current one p Preview the destination ( Enter follows, y copies, Esc cancels) E Open the full warning or error message that the footer truncated (the E in Error: is underlined) S Open the curl download popup for the current PDF page (PDF viewer only; does nothing elsewhere) y Copy selected text, the current URL when nothing is selected, the download curl command, or the whole active console section Y Copy the whole rendered page as plain text (no colors or browser chrome) for pasting into a bug report; the first line is a # metadata line with URL, sizes, zoom and modes Ctrl-Y Copy only the page rows visible at the current scroll, with the same metadata line H Session history t / Ctrl-T Open a new tab at the end with an empty URL prompt W / Ctrl-W Close the active tab and return to the previously used tab; closing the last tab exits without confirmation Ctrl-N / Ctrl-P (or Alt-Right / Alt-Left , l / h ) Next / previous tab without wrapping 1 - 9 / Alt-1 - Alt-9 Select a tab by its number in the tab strip ? Help page ! JavaScript console q / Ctrl-C Ask to confirm quitting ( y / Enter quits; any other key cancels) :q Exit immediately (in the : prompt) Esc Stop loading, a running script or a synchronous layout, leave a field, clear a selection, or show the exit hint Back and Forward remember the scroll position you last had on each history entry. In-page # links become history entries too, so h returns to where you were before following the anchor, and Forward returns to the anchored section. If only the fragment changes, the page is not reloaded. Zoom changes how many CSS pixels one terminal cell represents, from 25% to 200% in preset steps. Use -zoom N at launch to start at another level. It is not text scaling: glyphs stay one cell per character and lines keep their normal height, so text stays readable while fixed pixel widths, padding, borders, images and kitty scaled large headings grow or shrink. The CSS viewport shrinks or grows with the ratio, so responsive pages reflow and percentage columns keep filling the terminal. Zoom lasts for the session and applies to every page, including pages restored from the back/forward cache. The URL row always shows the current level as a clickable badge at its far right; clicking it opens a window where a preset can be chosen with the mouse, the arrow keys or the mouse wheel, then Enter . Console ! or the >_ footer icon opens the console. Inside it, : or Enter focuses the JavaScript input row, Tab / Shift-Tab or h / l switch sections, j / k and g / G scroll the active section, and y copies the whole active section (the logs, the raw page HTML, the request list, storage, or the Tools scrollback). Clicking a tab switches sections, clicking the input row focuses it, and the × in the console menu closes it; the wheel scrolls the active section. A JavaScript alert, confirm or prompt opened from the console is drawn over it and must be answered before the console takes input again. Esc leaves the input row first and closes the console on the next press; ! closes it immediately. URL prompt o , Ctrl-L , : or clicking the URL bar opens the prompt prefilled with the current URL and selected. Typing replaces the selection; Backspace clears it first. : is a Vim-style command prompt: q or :q plus Enter exits immediately, without the confirmation that q asks for. History of earlier URLs and search terms is kept in memory for the session. Key Action Up / Ctrl-P Recall the previous URL/search entry Down / Ctrl-N Recall the next entry, or the draft Left / Right Move one character Home / End Start / end Ctrl-B / Ctrl-F Move one character Alt-B / Alt-F Move one word Ctrl-Left / Ctrl-Right Move one word Alt-Left / Alt-Right Move one word Backspace / Delete Delete around the caret Alt-Backspace / Ctrl-Backspace Delete the previous word or URL segment Ctrl-W Delete the previous word or URL segment Alt-D / Alt-Delete / Ctrl-Delete Delete the next word plus separators Ctrl-A Select all Ctrl-K Delete to the end Ctrl-U Clear Ctrl-C / Esc Close without navigating Enter Navigate A mouse click places the caret. Shift+drag is left to the terminal for native selection. Input is resolved as a URL first, then through the -search fallback. Find / or Ctrl-F opens the incremental find bar. Typing searches case-insensitively and highlights every match. Enter confirms and closes the bar; n / N then move between matches, and the footer shows the match position with the n / N hint. Down / Up also move while the bar is open. Esc closes the bar but keeps highlights; a later Esc clears them. After the bar closes, f keeps its normal link-hint behavior. The find bar supports the same word-editing keys as the URL prompt. Link hints Press f to follow a link. It marks the links visible in the current viewport by highlighting letters from the link's own text with a yellow background and an underline. Form controls are labelled the same way: text fields focus, checkboxes and radios toggle, a select opens its options, multi-select options toggle, and buttons activate. The chosen control stays focused for Space/Enter and arrow or h/j/k/l keys. The page text is never changed. Up to 26 links the first letter not already used by another link is highlighted (case-insensitive), so Documentation uses D and a later Dog uses o if d is taken. On link-heavy pages a hint whose first letter is unique still shows only that one letter; hints sharing a first letter use two adjacent letters, with no gap between them. Type the highlighted letters to follow the link; either case works. Press F instead of f to open the chosen link in a background tab, or p to see the resolved destination highlighted in yellow in the footer and confirm with Enter or y before navigating; Esc or n cancels. If several labels share a first letter, the others disappear, the typed letter is no longer highlighted, and only the remaining letters stay marked. Unknown letters are ignored. Esc cancels hint mode, and scrolling with the wheel or scrollbar also dismisses the hints. Links outside the viewport are never labeled. A link with no text, such as an image link, gets free label letters drawn inside its box without brackets. While hints are shown, images are rendered as half-block art so the terminal's inline image protocol does not cover the hint letters. Forms Tab / Shift-Tab move through links and form controls in document order. A focused checkbox, radio, button or link is activated with Space / Enter ; arrows change radio groups, single selects and ranges, and move the active option in a multi-select, where Space toggles it. On radios, selects and ranges, h / l work like Left/Right and j / k like Down/Up. Click a text input or textarea to focus it and place the caret at the clicked character, then type; click the input row of a JavaScript prompt dialog the same way. The focused field shows a block caret. Key Action Left / Right Move by character Home / End Start / end of line Up / Down Move between lines in a textarea Alt-B / Alt-F Move by word Alt/Ctrl+Backspace , Ctrl-W Delete the previous word Alt-D / Alt-Delete / Ctrl-Delete Delete the next word Backspace Delete backwards Delete Delete forwards Ctrl-A Select all; typing, Backspace or Delete replaces the whole value Ctrl-K Delete to the end Ctrl-U Clear Enter Submit (or insert a newline in a textarea) Esc Leave the field Checkboxes and radios toggle on click. A <select> opens a popup; use Up / Down and Enter , the mouse wheel or pointer hover to move the highlight, or click an option. <select multiple> renders as a list box; clicking an option toggles it. A file input opens a notice that uploads are unsupported. Mouse Hover a link to show its target in the footer. Hovering also applies CSS :hover and fires mouse-over/enter events, so hover menus open and stay open while moving between padded items; style updates wait briefly for the pointer to settle. Leaving the page clears the hover state, and wheel scrolling never blocks on a hover rebuild. Drag to select page text; y copies it through OSC 52. With no selection, y copies the current URL. Click a link to follow it; a download link opens the curl popup without fetching, except a .pdf download, which opens in the viewer. Middle-click a link to open it in a background tab after the current one. Middle-click a tab to close it; closing the last tab exits without confirmation. Click the URL bar to open the prompt; click outside to close it. Click in the prompt to move the caret. Click the zoom badge at the right end of the URL row to open the zoom window and choose a preset. Click ← , → , ⟳ , ⌂ , History, Help or ⚙ in the toolbar. Right click a link to copy its URL (OSC 52); elsewhere it does nothing. Any click dismisses the quit confirmation, like Esc . The mouse back/forward buttons navigate. Click Dark:on / Dark:off , Reader:on / Reader:off , JavaScript:on / JavaScript:off or the >_ icon in the footer. The wheel scrolls three lines; drag the right-edge scrollbar to jump. Shift+mouse reports from the terminal are ignored so the terminal can keep a native selection over the URL. Terminal-native selection needs the terminal's Shift override. Terminal support MOB3 enables the kitty keyboard protocol in detected kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and foot terminals. That makes Esc , modified keys, Shift-Tab and key releases reliable. Multiplexers (tmux, screen, zellij) are excluded because their forwarding is inconsistent.","headings":[{"id":"page-navigation","text":"Page navigation"},{"id":"console","text":"Console"},{"id":"url-prompt","text":"URL prompt"},{"id":"find","text":"Find"},{"id":"link-hints","text":"Link hints"},{"id":"forms","text":"Forms"},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse"},{"id":"terminal-support","text":"Terminal support"}]},{"path":"docs/security.html","title":"Security and privacy — MOB3 documentation","text":"Security and privacy Warning: MOB3 is not a secure browser. It has no sandboxing, process or site isolation, CSP enforcement, certificate UI, phishing protection or security audit. Treat every page as code running beside the browser. Do not use MOB3 for passwords, accounts, payments or confidential data. What it does not do No sandbox, site isolation or permission prompts. No memory, site or OS isolation. By default tabs are separate engine child processes with their own JavaScript runtime and history, over parent-owned shared origin storage, but they still trust the same parent host and protocol values; iframes remain inert stubs. The child process is fault isolation, not a security boundary, and -process=single puts every tab back in one address space. An engine bug or a sobek escape can still cross tab boundaries. No CSP enforcement, certificate warnings or certificate management UI. -certs changes which root bundle is trusted; it is not certificate inspection, revocation checking or pinning. No phishing, malware, tracker or fingerprinting protection. No proxy configuration UI. Standard HTTP_PROXY , HTTPS_PROXY and NO_PROXY environment variables are honored by the host transport; see Proxies . No private/incognito concept: every session is ephemeral. Tabs may run in separate engine processes by default, but that is fault isolation only, not memory or site isolation. Need more isolation? MOB3 does not sandbox pages itself. If you need a more isolated environment, run MOB3 in a container or a dedicated virtual machine with only the network and filesystem access you intend. Docker is a common way to do this, but container and VM configuration is outside this project's support scope. Tab isolation (iframes are inert) Interactive tabs run one engine child process per tab by default, and an MCP automation session runs its engine in a child too; -process=single keeps them inside one Go process. The process layout and spawn handshake are described in the process model . This is fault isolation, not a sandbox: A tab is one protocol session with its own engine session, page, layout, JavaScript runtime and back/forward history. localStorage is shared per origin across tabs; sessionStorage is per tab and origin; cookies remain host-owned and domain-scoped. Single mode uses one shared budget for all JS runtimes, page caches, image/SVG work and the MCP engine. Per-tab mode gives each tab child and the MCP engine child its own process-wide -mem-limit cap. Zoom stays browser-global. Only the active tab draws. A background tab keeps loading and updates its own state, history and title, but its timers and deferred image work pause. A crashed MCP engine does not stop the MCP server, but the server does not respawn it: later tool calls report the crash until the client restarts the server. No DOM or sobek value crosses a tab boundary. This is logical isolation, not memory or site isolation. A compromised engine or a JavaScript-engine escape can read across tabs, and there is no OS sandbox or Spectre mitigation. Iframes are a permanent non-goal. Iframe elements have inert stub documents, contentDocument is null even for a same-origin frame, frameElement is null, and there is no DOM bridge. There is no child browsing context, frame tree or cross-frame DOM access, and none is planned. No persistence The running browser does not create or update configuration, history, cookie, cache, log or download files. Closing MOB3 saves nothing. History, cookies, localStorage / sessionStorage , the image cache and all JavaScript state live in memory only. The download popup only shows a curl command; it writes nothing. The binary opens no application files for reading or writing: there are no config, bookmark, certificate, cache, history or download files. It writes nothing to disk and never probes or reads a page, bundle or path. OS-level reads by Go's TLS, DNS and timezone libraries are not application storage. By default TLS roots come from the platform store; -certs can switch to the CA bundle compiled into the binary. No PEM file is read. The memory footer uses Go's runtime statistics for the active engine process; no /proc file is sampled. Per-tab mode therefore shows the active tab's child process, not the whole browser. Cookies and storage Cookies are session-only and shared by page loads, resources, XHR, fetch and document.cookie . HttpOnly cookies are hidden from JavaScript, and a script cannot replace or delete a server-set HttpOnly cookie. Explicit SameSite values are enforced: Strict cookies are sent only to the same site, Lax also on top-level GET / HEAD navigations, and None only when Secure over HTTPS. A cookie with no SameSite keeps the legacy send-everywhere behavior. Non-GET form submissions carry the submitting page's Origin , so a server can reject cross-site posts. A Secure cookie is accepted only over HTTPS, and the __Secure- / __Host- prefixes are enforced: the former needs Secure , the latter needs Secure , Path=/ and no Domain . localStorage and sessionStorage are in-memory and do not persist across restarts: localStorage is shared per origin across tabs, while sessionStorage is per tab and origin. Each area is capped at 5 MiB and the whole browser context at 32 MiB. Opaque origins get a private area that is not shared with any other document. One cookie is capped at 4 KiB accounting size, a domain at 180 cookies, and the jar at 1024 cookies / 4 MiB; a cookie that would exceed a bound is not stored. Clipboard Clipboard use is write-only and user-initiated: y / Y / Ctrl-Y , a right-click link URL, console y , or the download prompt write through OSC 52. Page JavaScript cannot read or write the clipboard ( navigator.clipboard is absent and document.execCommand(\"copy\") is false). The engine emits plain text; the front end allows one clipboard event per copy action, drops unsolicited events, strips terminal and display-format controls, caps the payload at 100 KiB and reports \"Clipboard unavailable\" when the terminal write fails. MOB3 never reads the clipboard. Terminal support for OSC 52 still varies. Cross-origin requests Pages can load cross-origin scripts, styles and images like a normal browser, and share the cookie jar for those requests. JavaScript fetch and XMLHttpRequest allow same-origin requests and only simple CORS cross-origin requests with a matching Access-Control-Allow-Origin ; preflight and credentialed cross-origin requests are unsupported. A document loaded from a public http / https address cannot fetch subresources, fetch or XHR from loopback, private, link-local, CGNAT, 0.0.0.0/8 or reserved addresses. Literal local hosts and local names are rejected before the request and on every redirect hop; names that only resolve locally are rejected after DNS resolution, and every connection is re-checked before it is reused from the pool. Only the final document address decides local access, so a public page reached through a local redirect hop is still public. Documents that themselves loaded from a local address keep access, and top-level navigation is not blocked. Blocked requests appear as ERR in the console request list. MOB3 upgrades HTTP image requests on HTTPS pages to HTTPS and does not retry them over HTTP; HTTP scripts, stylesheets, modules, @import , XHR/fetch and secure-subresource redirects to HTTP are blocked. IP-literal image hosts are blocked rather than upgraded. Top-level navigation to HTTP remains allowed. Blocked sites and challenges On 401/403/429/503 responses, MOB3 looks for common anti-bot/CAPTCHA signatures and shows a generic “Site blocked” page with the reason, status and URL. The site's challenge scripts are not executed unless you follow the one-shot Try anyway link, or start with -ignore-blocks . Challenge pages can run arbitrary script after that bypass. MOB3 cannot complete most browser checks, and it is not a security boundary. Local files and downloads Local file pages are not supported. file: URLs are rejected, bare paths are never probed on disk, and every document comes from http , https or about: . data: is accepted only as an inline subresource (images, stylesheets, scripts), never as a document. Remote pages therefore cannot use the viewport, a stylesheet, a script, an image or XHR to read a local file. MOB3 never saves downloads. A download link opens a popup with a ready-to-copy curl command without fetching the file, except for a .pdf download, which is fetched and shown in the text viewer. Running the curl command is your choice and outside MOB3. TLS trust store HTTPS verification uses the platform root store by default. If the platform provides no roots at all, the Mozilla NSS bundle compiled into the binary from golang.org/x/crypto/x509roots/fallback is used automatically. -certs=embedded selects that same bundle explicitly, replacing a stale platform store. No certificate file is read. The embedded bundle keeps Mozilla's distrust-after dates, so a constrained root stops validating certificates issued after that date. Revocation is still not checked in any mode, and there is no certificate UI or warning flow. Proxies The host transport uses Go's http.ProxyFromEnvironment , so HTTP_PROXY / HTTPS_PROXY / NO_PROXY work without a CLI flag or UI. HTTP proxies, HTTPS proxies and SOCKS5 proxy URLs are supported by the transport. The variables are read at process start. With a proxy, the proxy resolves and contacts the destination. The local-network guard cannot inspect that target, so the proxy is a deliberate exception to the private-network check. The proxy can see plain HTTP traffic and destination hostnames; HTTPS remains end-to-end encrypted unless the proxy terminates TLS. Proxy credentials in environment variables are visible to child processes and may be visible through process-environment inspection. This is best-effort networking, not a security boundary. Automation -mcp runs a local, unauthenticated automation server over stdin/stdout. Any process connected to that stdin/stdout can navigate, click, type and evaluate JavaScript in the page. Do not expose the MCP server as a network service and do not connect untrusted clients. Sessions and storage remain memory-only. Media decoding Images and inline SVG are treated as untrusted input. Responses are capped at 12 MB, raster images above 25 megapixels are rejected before decoding, and SVG rasterization is capped at 1024 px. Decoder dependencies are kept at patched releases and scanned with govulncheck ; a panic inside a raster or SVG decoder is contained and shown as a broken image instead of crashing the browser. SVG strokes with a pathological dash pattern fall back to a solid stroke, and a stroke outline beyond a fixed point budget is dropped. These limits bound resource use and crashes, not the general risk of rendering attacker-controlled content. CSS selector matching memoizes repeated ancestor states and runs under a per-selector or per-query step budget. A pathological selector is treated as non-matching instead of freezing the engine, and the style deadline and script abort flag are checked inside the match. UA rules have their own small budget, so exhausting author matching cannot expose script/style raw text. PDF responses are parsed in-repo rather than by a third-party renderer. The reader is bounded (500 pages, 4 seconds of parse time, 4 MiB of extracted text, 32 MiB per decoded stream, 64 MiB of retained page content), stops on the requesting page's cancellation, rejects encrypted documents, and contains panics, so a malformed file degrades to a note instead of a crash. It never rasterizes a page and writes nothing to disk. A panic in the engine goroutine is recovered by the session transport and shown in the crashed tab, and an engine that stops answering the liveness probe is force-killed and shown the same way. Fatal runtime errors, such as a stack overflow, are not recoverable: per-tab mode contains one to the tab's child process and shows its captured message, while -process=single terminates the browser and may leave the terminal in raw or alternate-screen mode. Like the rest of the engine it is not audited and is not a security boundary. Memory and caches The back/forward history-cache pool is 12% of -mem-limit / GOMEMLIMIT (or 128 MB when unlimited), divided across active tabs. Each tab evicts its least recently used pages first, and a page heavier than half its share is never cached. When a heavy cached page is evicted, one collection plus scavenge runs between frames so its memory returns to the OS. The separate subresource cache keys CSS, JavaScript and raw image bodies by top-level site as well as URL, so one site cannot reuse another site's cached response, and a response that varies on Cookie (or Vary: * ) is not cached at all. Decoded images remain keyed by URL. -mem-limit sets an optional soft limit on the Go heap, making the runtime collect harder as memory approaches it. It is off by default ( 0 ), so memory is unlimited and any GOMEMLIMIT environment setting applies. Single-mode tabs divide the image/SVG pools (12% and 3% of the limit when set, with conservative fallbacks otherwise) because they share one process. With -process=per-tab , every tab child is its own process and receives the full -mem-limit , as does the parent, so the browser total is not bounded by one limit. These limits are best-effort resource bounds, not a sandbox. JavaScript safety limits Each script and callback runs under a 2-second sobek interrupt watchdog and a per-runtime absolute heap ceiling drawn from the shared JS pool (25% of -mem-limit / GOMEMLIMIT , else 256 MB, divided across active sessions). The interrupt is observed by bytecode, not by a native regular-expression match, so catastrophic backtracking can exceed it until the liveness probe force-stops the tab (see the known limitations). One sampler per active runtime checks the ceiling every 75 ms and interrupts only a call that is running, so a page cannot reset its budget on every timer callback. A page's load-time scripts also share an 8-second budget and a 2,000-script cap, and Esc or Ctrl-C interrupts the script currently blocking the loop and cancels its in-flight requests; closing the tab does the same before releasing it. Timer counts and per-tick callbacks are capped. Go-side copies of page data are capped too: the console log at 8 KiB per line and 512 KiB total, DOM text and attribute values at 1 MiB each and 64 MiB per runtime, script-visible nodes at 200,000, and request-log URLs at 2 KiB. Interactive alert / confirm / prompt dialogs are limited to 20 per page load and 8 KiB each; later calls return the non-interactive defaults, so a script cannot reopen a modal forever. These limits protect against runaway scripts, not against malicious pages in general.","headings":[{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do"},{"id":"need-more-isolation","text":"Need more isolation?"},{"id":"tab-isolation-iframes-are-inert","text":"Tab isolation (iframes are inert)"},{"id":"no-persistence","text":"No persistence"},{"id":"cookies-and-storage","text":"Cookies and storage"},{"id":"clipboard","text":"Clipboard"},{"id":"cross-origin-requests","text":"Cross-origin requests"},{"id":"blocked-sites-and-challenges","text":"Blocked sites and challenges"},{"id":"local-files-and-downloads","text":"Local files and downloads"},{"id":"tls-trust-store","text":"TLS trust store"},{"id":"proxies","text":"Proxies"},{"id":"automation","text":"Automation"},{"id":"media-decoding","text":"Media decoding"},{"id":"memory-and-caches","text":"Memory and caches"},{"id":"javascript-safety-limits","text":"JavaScript safety limits"}]},{"path":"docs/sites.html","title":"Site support — MOB3 documentation","text":"Site support MOB3 is a content browser, not a full browser engine. It is happiest with text-focused, server-rendered pages and gets rough on modern JavaScript apps. Works well Documentation, blogs, news articles, wikis, forums, changelogs and old or server-rendered HTML. Headings, paragraphs, lists, links, tables, <pre> blocks, images and common presentational markup. Responsive pages that use @media width queries, CSS cascade layers ( @layer ), basic flex rows and column flex media boxes, including framework columns with percentage offsets and the common box-sizing: inherit reset, basic grid tracks with explicit grid-row / grid-area placement and horizontal justify-items / justify-self , simple calc() (including viewport units), min() / max() / clamp() and viewport-unit sizing, aspect-ratio ::before / ::after boxes and literal pseudo-element text, images sized with object-fit , CSS background-image photos on block containers, component <style> rules injected by scripts after load, forms and simple JavaScript DOM effects. Older pages that declare a single-byte charset (Windows-1250/1251/1253/1257 and ISO-8859-2/5/7 and similar) are decoded correctly from the Content-Type header or <meta charset> . Fixed-width desktop layouts keep their declared canvas width even when it is wider than the terminal, so columns stay in place and the page clips at the right edge, like a browser window narrower than the page, instead of collapsing into one reflowed column. Classic bundles that only need platform APIs to exist: IntersectionObserver and ResizeObserver (one synthetic entry per target), NodeList , EventTarget , CSS.escape , customElements , ReadableStream , Path2D , Web Animations stubs and inert Worker s. ES module scripts run with their relative imports, dynamic import() returns a real namespace, and import.meta.url reports the page URL. Responsive and lazy images: srcset candidates, <picture> sources and common data-src / data-srcset placeholders are selected, and images added or changed by scripts are loaded after the page runs. Reader mode ( R or -reader ) for long articles. PDF documents A PDF response opens in a built-in, text-only viewer: every page is extracted and shown as one continuous document where each page ends with its Page n caption above an underscore rule. The reading column is centered and every page is padded to the same height, so the document scrolls like a printed book. Text keeps its column alignment, so tables and multi-column pages stay readable, and the result supports find, selection, -dump and -png like any other page. The footer shows Page n/N and a clickable (S -> Download name) hint; S opens the same curl popup. The viewer never rasterizes a page. Figures, images, charts, annotations and links are not shown, scanned PDFs have no text to show, and encrypted (password-protected) documents are refused. -no-pdf turns the viewer off and restores the download prompt; an unreadable document always offers the same curl command. Often rough Single-page applications, heavy shadow DOM, custom elements and CSS-in-JS. Sites that require a browser check, CAPTCHA, unusual TLS or a specific certificate. Complex grid/flex layouts, sticky positioning beyond a top offset, nested z-index stacking contexts, full float behavior, animations and non-text generated content ( attr() , counters, images, icon-font private-use glyphs). Visual results from canvas, WebGL, video and WebAudio. Multipart/file-upload forms. Not supported Import map scopes, workers, service workers, WebSocket, IndexedDB, WebRTC, geolocation, clipboard APIs, notifications and browser payment APIs. Async generator syntax ( async function* ) and for await : the script containing them does not parse, so it is skipped. CSP enforcement and certificate UI. Environment proxy variables are supported; there is no proxy configuration UI. Browser pixel screenshots for automation; the MCP screenshot tool exports the terminal-cell raster instead. Tips -no-js shows the no-script fallback, which is sometimes more useful. J toggles scripting and reloads the page for the rest of the session. R extracts the main article and removes navigation, sidebars and images. D asks sites for prefers-color-scheme: dark and keeps an author dark palette when the page already renders a dark background. -layout-budget low can keep a very large or generated page responsive. -dump -width 120 -no-color is handy for comparing layout without a terminal.","headings":[{"id":"works-well","text":"Works well"},{"id":"pdf-documents","text":"PDF documents"},{"id":"often-rough","text":"Often rough"},{"id":"not-supported","text":"Not supported"},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips"}]},{"path":"docs/use-cases.html","title":"Use cases — MOB3 documentation","text":"Use cases MOB3 is a terminal browser with a headless MCP mode, so it fits anywhere a graphical browser does not: SSH sessions, containers, CI runners, remote servers and AI agents. It is small and text-focused, not a full browser engine, so the best results come from content pages, docs and server-rendered HTML; see site support . Remember that MOB3 is not a secure browser : no sandboxing, no site isolation, no CSP. Use it for reading and automation, never for passwords, accounts, payments or confidential data. See security . Developers and sysadmins Terminal browsing — browse websites directly from SSH sessions, servers, containers and minimal Linux environments. Headless website testing — load pages, execute JavaScript, inspect rendered content and verify website behavior without Chromium. Web scraping — extract text, links, metadata and other content from JavaScript-driven pages. Website debugging — inspect how HTML, CSS and JavaScript render without opening a graphical browser. Screenshot generation — capture web pages as PNG images directly from scripts or the command line ( mob3 -png ... > page.png ). CI/CD integration — include lightweight browser checks in automated pipelines. Server-side automation — automate web interactions on remote machines without a desktop environment. AI agents and MCP Start with mob3 -mcp and the automation reference . AI web browsing — give AI agents the ability to browse and interact with real websites through MCP. AI research assistants — let agents navigate websites, follow links, collect information and summarize findings. Autonomous web automation — allow agents to fill out forms, click buttons, navigate pages and execute workflows. Local AI browser — provide a browser that runs locally, without sending browser activity through a third-party automation service. Agentic workflows — integrate web browsing into multi-step AI workflows and scripts. Website inspection by AI — let agents inspect rendered pages, discover interactive elements and understand page structure. Everyday terminal users SSH browsing — browse the web from remote servers without installing a desktop environment. Distraction-free browsing — access websites without the overhead of a graphical browser. Low-resource environments — browse on machines with limited memory, CPU or graphical capabilities. The project aims to cover most of the features people use while using far less CPU and memory than a mainstream browser. Privacy-conscious browsing — keep browsing history, cookies, storage and caches in memory only; nothing is written to disk. Inline image viewing — view web images directly in compatible terminals with -graphics=auto|kitty|iterm|blocks . Automation and integration Shell scripting — fetch and interact with websites as part of shell scripts, for example with mob3 -dump URL . Monitoring — periodically inspect web pages and detect content changes. Documentation lookup — browse online documentation directly from development environments. Link checking — visit URLs and inspect whether pages render correctly. Content conversion — turn rendered web pages into text or screenshots for further processing. Custom tooling — build other applications and agents on top of MOB3's browser capabilities.","headings":[{"id":"developers-and-sysadmins","text":"Developers and sysadmins"},{"id":"ai-agents-and-mcp","text":"AI agents and MCP"},{"id":"everyday-terminal-users","text":"Everyday terminal users"},{"id":"automation-and-integration","text":"Automation and integration"}]}];
